From: Joel Winarske <joel.winarske@linux.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Do not take the listeners mutex in a desktop current_user

Auth::current_user() on desktop adds a listener and waits for it, every
call, to wait out the load of the persistent cache.  Adding a listener takes
listeners_mutex.  NotifyAuthStateListeners() holds listeners_mutex while it
calls each listener, and Firestore's credentials provider is one: its
OnAuthStateChanged takes the provider's mutex.  The provider's GetToken holds
that mutex and calls current_user() through IsSignedIn().

So a sign-out on one thread and a Firestore stream asking for a token on
another deadlock, each holding the lock the other wants.  An app that signs
out while a listen is starting hangs for good.

Once the cache has loaded the listener is called back at once and there is
nothing to wait for, so skip it then and read the user.  The flag is written
under listeners_mutex and is now atomic so it can be read without.  Before
the load the listener stays; Firestore registers its own listener only after
its first current_user() returns, so it cannot be notified in that window.

Upstream: https://github.com/firebase/firebase-cpp-sdk/issues/1972

---
 auth/src/data.h                  |  6 ++++--
 auth/src/desktop/auth_desktop.cc | 25 ++++++++++++++++---------
 2 files changed, 20 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

diff --git a/auth/src/data.h b/auth/src/data.h
index 58098bb..6e39982 100644
--- a/auth/src/data.h
+++ b/auth/src/data.h
@@ -17,6 +17,7 @@
 #ifndef FIREBASE_AUTH_SRC_DATA_H_
 #define FIREBASE_AUTH_SRC_DATA_H_
 
+#include <atomic>
 #include <string>
 
 #include "app/src/include/firebase/internal/mutex.h"
@@ -170,8 +171,9 @@ struct AuthData {
   // Tracks if the Id Token listener is expecting a callback to occur.
   bool expect_id_token_listener_callback;
 
-  // Tracks if the persistent cache load is pending.
-  bool persistent_cache_load_pending;
+  // Tracks if the persistent cache load is pending. Written under
+  // `listeners_mutex`; atomic so that `current_user()` can read it without.
+  std::atomic<bool> persistent_cache_load_pending;
 
   // Mutex protecting `expect_id_token_listener_callback`
   Mutex expect_id_token_mutex;
diff --git a/auth/src/desktop/auth_desktop.cc b/auth/src/desktop/auth_desktop.cc
index a5b59a8..af0f5bf 100644
--- a/auth/src/desktop/auth_desktop.cc
+++ b/auth/src/desktop/auth_desktop.cc
@@ -541,15 +541,22 @@ class CurrentUserBlockListener : public firebase::auth::AuthStateListener {
 User Auth::current_user() {
   if (!auth_data_) return User(nullptr);
 
-  // Add a listener and wait for the first trigger.
-  CurrentUserBlockListener listener;
-  AddAuthStateListener(&listener);
-  // If the persistent cache has not be loaded, this would wait until the
-  // loading is finished and OnAuthStateChanged() is triggered.
-  // If it has been loaded, OnAuthStateChanged() should be triggered recursively
-  // and synchronously when AddAuthStateListener() is called.
-  listener.WaitForEvent();
-  RemoveAuthStateListener(&listener);
+  // Once the persistent cache has loaded there is nothing to wait for, and
+  // the listener below would only take `listeners_mutex` to be called back at
+  // once. Taking it here deadlocks: NotifyAuthStateListeners() holds it while
+  // it calls Firestore's credentials provider, which takes its own mutex, and
+  // the provider calls this holding that mutex.
+  if (auth_data_->persistent_cache_load_pending) {
+    // Add a listener and wait for the first trigger.
+    CurrentUserBlockListener listener;
+    AddAuthStateListener(&listener);
+    // If the persistent cache has not be loaded, this would wait until the
+    // loading is finished and OnAuthStateChanged() is triggered.
+    // If it has been loaded, OnAuthStateChanged() should be triggered
+    // recursively and synchronously when AddAuthStateListener() is called.
+    listener.WaitForEvent();
+    RemoveAuthStateListener(&listener);
+  }
 
   {
     MutexLock lock(auth_data_->future_impl.mutex());
